WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.953
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:07.370
VIVIAN SONG: In
this video, I will

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:10.850
be going over Goodie Bag number
eight, which is about reactions

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.350
and reaction rates.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.240
What you'll need are pH strips,
small measuring cups, stirrers,

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.220
gloves, citric powder, cups,
a scale, and seashells.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:25.790
No sea animals were harmed in
the making of this Goodie bag.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.880
The objectives are to understand
how climate change has

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.950
caused ocean acidification.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.040
Assess the order of the
reaction from empirical data.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.190
Learn how temperature can
affect the reaction rate.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.520
And measure the pH of a
reaction using pH strips.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.040
Some conceptual
questions to think about

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.900
are, what is the reaction order?

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.610
And how does the
reaction rate change

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:51.720
if the temperature increases or
if we have outside pH effects?

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.160
Now, in this Goodie bag, we will
be exploring the application

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.830
of reaction rates in
ocean acidification

00:00:57.830 --> 00:00:59.480
due to climate
change and what that

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.370
means for many sea animals.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.080
As you can see from
this graph, there

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.650
is a strong positive correlation
between increasing carbon

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.720
dioxide levels in
the atmosphere, which

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.360
is marked by the red,
and decreasing pH

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.170
in the ocean, which corresponds
to increasing acidity, as seen

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.400
in the green.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.520
What exactly is going on
in terms of the chemistry?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.390
First, carbon dioxide from the
air dissolves into the oceans

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.400
and reacts with water
to form carbonic acid.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.330
Then the carbonic
acid donates a proton.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.120
Finally, calcium
carbonate from seashells

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.940
reacts with excess
protons in solution

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.860
to formed bicarbonate ions.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.210
What does this all mean?

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.210
Seashells are dissolving
in a more acidic ocean,

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.010
which is very sad.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.990
One final note about the slide.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.450
I've only drawn the
forward reaction arrows.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.090
But these reactions are
actually reversible.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.670
So there should also
be backward arrows.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.740
However, the forward
reactions are

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.180
more dominant to the
reverse reactions.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.140
So the net product is that
of the forward reaction.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.120
Now, let's dive
into the experiment

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.700
and see this happen
in real life.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.620
First, take out one
of your seashells

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.490
and weigh it with your scale.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.240
Next, put on your
elegant nitrile gloves.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.630
And take a large plastic cup and
have it filled with tap water

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.070
about halfway.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.530
Measure out two scoops
of the citric powder

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.840
using the small but
accurate measuring cup.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.430
And put it into the water.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.410
Then stir while
using the stirrer

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.590
until all of the citric
powder is fully dissolved.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.060
It might take a
minute of stirring,

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.190
but the water should
look pretty clear.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.150
After that, you can
use your pH strip

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.360
to measure the pH
of the solution.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.500
Have a timer ready.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.480
And when you're ready,
place the shells

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.580
that you weighed into
the low pH solution.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.080
And record what you observe.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.990
After 20 minutes, take out
the shells with your gloves

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.090
on and record the exact time.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.940
Let the shells dry for
maybe half an hour or so so

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.710
that all the water
has evaporated.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:53.270
And then measure the weight.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.220
Repeat this step
two more times so

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.390
that you have data points of the
mass of the shell at zero, 20,

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.010
and 40 minutes.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:04.040
The pH that I measured
was a pH of 2,

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.647
as you can see using
this pH tester.

00:03:07.647 --> 00:03:09.230
While doing the
experiment, you should

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.540
see that the sea
shells are dissolving

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.930
in the acidic solution.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:17.840
And here, I've captured a
hyper-lapse of this happening.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.060
The focus of our
experiment is to figure out

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.310
the reaction rate of the
dissolution of calcium

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.030
carbonate.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.090
As a reminder, the reaction
rate is equal to some rate

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.480
constant times the
concentration of the calcium

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.800
ions to the n power times the
concentration of carbonate ions

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.350
to the n power, where n plus n
is equal to the reaction order.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.580
And that is determined
empirically from the data

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.440
that we've gathered.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.190
As you can see from the data,
this reaction is 0th order.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.590
I plotted the concentration
of carbonate ions

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.570
on the y-axis, which
you can figure out,

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.120
assuming that you poured
0.2 liters of water

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.120
in your experiment,
and time on the x-axis.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.650
Because this plot looks linear,
the rate is constant over time

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.420
and does not depend on the
concentration of carbonate ions

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.770
already in solution.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.350
Thus, this is a
0th order reaction.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.470
You could also imagine the
same plot with calcium ions

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:12.740
since we know that the calcium
ions and carbonate ions exist

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.620
in a one-to-one molarity ratio.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.420
In summary, the
reaction rate order

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.829
is determined empirically.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:22.040
And the reaction rate can
depend on temperature and pH.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.130
Now, we didn't have time to
do this pH and temperature

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.980
dependence experiment
in the video.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.510
But you can imagine how
temperature and pH might

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.905
affect your reaction rate.

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:34.250
And finally, ocean acidification
linked to climate change

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.440
is causing our seashells to
dissolve, which is very sad.

